      "type": "thinking",
      "text": "**Investigating Visual Invisibility**\n\nOkay, I've dug into the \"Disciplinas.tsx\" code. It seems the date input, which uses the calendar functionality, is embedded within a grid. My current focus is on pinpointing why it's not visible in the \"Disciplinas\" page within the 'topics' section. I suspect the issue is CSS-related, specifically styling, or possibly a visibility property setting the input's rendering to hidden or transparent. I will be looking closely at the styling of the grid container.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Invisibility Cause**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the date input's invisibility within `Disciplinas.tsx`. I see that the input itself *should* be visible and styled. I reviewed prior turn's code again and realize `appearance-none` was likely not added in error, suggesting that the browser is rendering it as its default (invisible). The CSS class looks correct, but browser defaults still cause problems. I'm exploring if browser-specific rendering is overriding our styling, since the user said invisible, not just poorly styled.\n\n\n**Refining Date Picker Clarity**\n\nI'm now considering the invisibility of the date picker. Initial code inspections seem correct on the styling. It might not be \"invisible\" per se, but perhaps the date picker icon isn't obvious, especially without a click. Tailwind resets could also be involved. I'm investigating how browser defaults or styling could make the picker less apparent, such as the color, or the picker itself not showing without a click.\n\n\n**Enhancing Date Input Visuals**\n\nI'm now focusing on improving the date input's visual prominence. I'm adding `cursor-pointer` to visually indicate clickability. Also, I'm verifying the input's text is visible and tweaking the styles for better contrast across browsers. The `appearance-none` issue seems resolved, but I am now focused on a custom background and color scheme to make it more obvious.\n\n\n**Optimizing Date Picker Visibility**\n\nI'm now implementing visibility improvements for the date input. I will begin by making the date input container more distinct visually. Next, I'm forcing `color-scheme: light` and also adding a `cursor-pointer` to improve the interaction. I've also re-confirmed that the text rendering is visible, and will be re-validating the timezone fix.\n\n\n**Improving Date Field Visibility**\n\nI've been looking over the code again for the date handling. The date conversion, especially with timezones, could be problematic, so I'll create a date format helper to avoid those issues. I also realize that the user's \"invisible\" might be a visual cue issue with the browser's native date input and not CSS. I'm focusing on adding a Lucide icon *inside* the input to make it more obvious to the user that it's a clickable date input on browsers which may not provide a default visible picker.\n\n\n**Enhancing Date Field Aesthetics**\n\nI'm now focusing on improving the date input's visual clarity. I'm adding additional styling to the input container to enhance its appearance and ensure it stands out. I am adding a `color-scheme` to the input to guarantee a consistent appearance. The timezone issue should be resolved now that the date conversion is properly considered.\n\n\n**Refining Date Storage Integrity**\n\nI'm now focusing on date handling to prevent potential timezone-related inconsistencies. I'm exploring the best approach for storing dates, either using `getUTC...` methods or setting a consistent mid-day time (like `T12:00:00`). Specifically, I'm working on refactoring date loading logic, ensuring that we load dates correctly regardless of how they are stored (either UTC or local) and how they may be displayed. I'm also preparing to integrate a visual fix for the date input.\n\n\n\n",
