      "type": "thinking",
      "text": "**Defining Alert Thresholds**\n\nI'm currently focused on defining the specific alerts for failed login attempts, as requested. I've mapped out the desired alert triggers: one after three failures (indicating limited attempts remaining), and another when the user is blocked after five attempts. I am considering different ways to display these alerts.\n\n\n**Refining Failure Alerts**\n\nI'm now revising the login component's error handling. The existing failed attempt counter in local storage is a good start. I'm implementing new specific alerts. After the third failed attempt, a warning will appear. The error message is updated at the fifth failure, as the account will be locked. These messages will provide clear guidance to the user on their next steps.\n\n\n**Verifying Application Logic**\n\nI'm now deep-diving into the application's core logic, especially concerning the login process. The `Login.tsx` component is my primary focus. I'm implementing the specific alert logic based on failed login attempts, ensuring users receive timely warnings. I've confirmed that `App.tsx` already contains the `window.location.reload()` fix, so no changes are required there. I'm checking the current logic for failure and will implement the alerts at attempts 3 and 5.\n\n\n**Implementing Alert Text**\n\nI've finalized the alert messages. The text is now specific and informative. For the third failed attempt, it will display, \"Atenção: Você errou a senha 3 vezes. Você tem apenas mais 2 tentativas antes do bloqueio definitivo da conta.\" On the fifth failure, it will show \"CONTA BLOQUEADA! Você excedeu o limite de 5 tentativas sem sucesso. Entre em contato com o suporte para desbloqueio.\" I've updated the error state variable accordingly. I'm satisfied with the phrasing and level of detail.\n\n\n**Confirming Account Lockout**\n\nI'm now implementing the account lockout alert. The system will now clearly display a \"CONTA BLOQUEADA!\" message after the fifth failed login attempt. This confirms that the alert logic is working as expected. I'm verifying the lockout functionality within `Login.tsx`, and ensuring the error state is correctly updated. I am also making sure that if the user clicks the \"restaurar backup\" button, `window.location.reload()` is invoked. I am checking `Profile.tsx` now.\n\n\n**Analyzing File Import**\n\nI'm now focusing on the file import functionality. I'm examining `handleFileChange` and the `handleImportData` function within `App.tsx`. While the `window.location.reload()` after import seems fine due to the asynchronous nature of the `FileReader`, I'm looking closer at the data validation and storage process within `handleImportData`. Specifically, I'm checking the atomic nature of writing the updated data to local storage after parsing the JSON file. I will check for errors.\n\n\n**Troubleshooting File Import**\n\nI'm now investigating potential causes for file import failure. The `if (user)` check seems correct, as the import functionality should only be accessible when a user is logged in, and therefore it seems OK. I'm focusing on the interaction between `onImport` (passed to `Profile`) and the rendering of `Profile` within `App` when the user is authenticated. I'm verifying that the file input in `Profile.tsx` correctly triggers the `onChange` event, and the import functions are actually called. I will use the `ref` I added to the button to check it.\n\n\n**Verifying Login Flow**\n\nI'm now revisiting the login flow to ensure the account lockout alert works consistently. The issue is likely unrelated to the file import, which is working. My focus has shifted back to the core login logic within `Login.tsx`. I need to confirm the correct display of the \"CONTA BLOQUEADA!\" message after five failed attempts. I'm carefully reviewing the conditional rendering to ensure the error state is accurately reflected.\n\n\n**Confirming Attempt Alerts**\n\nI'm now implementing the alert after the third login a