s source. The build log confirms it's using commit `9a3c7c4`. Could there be a mismatch between my current view and the committed version, especially around `services/geminiService.ts`? I will ensure file consistency across both, and then simplify configuration.\n\n\n**Confirming Error Persistence**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the explicit Rollup error. The error message is clear: `@google/generative-ai` is present in `geminiService.ts` within the build, despite my belief it's updated. I'll now explicitly replace the contents of this file to ensure there's no lingering trace of the outdated import. I'll also check if requestFramePermissions can be used, and keep it if the user wants it, even if the code doesn't actively use those permissions.\n\n\n**Confirming Hybrid Configuration**\n\nI've realized the problem lies in the conflicting configuration between the `importmap` in `index.html` and the `package.json` approach. Vite builds using `npm run build`, and ignores the `importmap` unless configured. The fix is straightforward. I will remove the importmap from `index.html` entirely and re-verify the imports.\n\n\n\n**Verifying Module Exports**\n\nI'm now methodically verifying the exports from `@google/genai`. It is essential that the internal logic is using the *correct* import names. I have to confirm that the service file actually uses the *old* import path, as indicated by the error message. I must meticulously eliminate any lingering references to `@google/generative-ai` to ensure the build doesn't fail due to an outdated import.\n\n\n\n**Refactoring Configuration Imports**\n\nI'm now focusing on eliminating all vestiges of the `importmap` from the `index.html` file, as the primary build configuration depends on the `package.json` approach for module resolution. This means verifying the core `geminiService.ts` code is using the *correct* import names. I have to confirm that all references to the deprecated import path are removed to resolve this build error. The g