  {
    "id": 407,
    "author": "model",
    "payload": {
      "type": "thinking",
      "text": "**Addressing the Error**\n\nI've determined the issue is a `TypeError: Failed to fetch`. It usually means network problems, CORS, or a bad URL. I previously set up a \"Local-First\" UI strategy to handle this gracefully. I don't want the user to get an alert.\n\n\n**Investigating the Root Cause**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into why the \"Failed to fetch\" error persists, even after implementing a \"Local-First\" UI strategy. The user's prompt emphasizes this specific error, which indicates that my prior attempt to address the issue may not be sufficient. I'm focusing on the `components/Disciplinas.tsx` file, where the error is logged, and the `lib/supabase.ts` file, where network connection is established. It seems the connection to the Supabase URL might be the initial problem. I am checking the error handling within the subject update functionality.\n\n\n**Refining Error Detection**\n\nI'm now refining the error detection strategy. It appears the initial check for \"Failed to fetch\" may be insufficient, as I'm still seeing the `TypeError` being logged. I'm focusing on the condition where `err.name` is `'TypeError'`. The error could be slightly different in its message or bubbling from another component. I'm expanding my investigation to `App.tsx`, `Simulados.tsx`, and `Revisao.tsx`, since these files also interact with Supabase and could be a source.\n\n\n**Centralizing Error Handling**\n\nI'm now thinking about a centralized approach for fetch errors. The goal is a service layer around Supabase calls, ensuring consistent \"offline/failed fetch\" logic across the app. This will be the most robust solution. I need to get rid of console errors and alerts when the network or Supabase is unstable. My plan is minimal updates. I want to refactor the supabase library.\n\n\n**Implementing Silent Error Handling**\n\nI'm now implementing silent error handling throughout the application. I will add a helper function, `isNetworkError(error)`, within `lib/supabase.ts`. I will update `components/Disciplinas.tsx`, `components/Simulados.tsx`, `components/Revisao.tsx`, and `components/Admin.tsx` to utilize this helper. Finally, I'm updating `App.tsx`'s `fetchUserData` and `fetchAllUsers` functions to be silent on network errors. Specifically focusing on `Disciplinas.tsx` since the user points to that component. I've reevaluated the invalid URL situation. I'll make the logic safer to ensure that no error alert occurs. The \"Erro de sincronização\" string also helped narrow the location to focus the efforts.\n\n\n**Expanding Error Handling**\n\nI'm now expanding error handling beyond the initial components. I will implement the centralized `isNetworkError` check and apply it across more components to silence network failures. I'm focusing on the specified components, updating the `try-catch` blocks in `Disciplinas.tsx` and `Simulados.tsx`. In `Simulados.tsx`, I'll remove the `alert`s for network issues. I'll extend this to `Revisao.tsx`, and `App.tsx` to handle similar network failure cases silently.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Error Condition**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on why the previous error handling didn't catch the *specific* error seen by the user: `\"Erro de sincronização: TypeError: Failed to fetch\"`. The code I wrote explicitly checks for `err.message === 'Failed to fetch' || err.name === 'TypeError'`, and the user's error suggests this condition wasn't met, meaning I need to examine how the error message itself is constructed and handled. It seems the issue is that I am logging the `err.message` from inside the `else` block, even though I've handled the error in other locations. I will also check the language to ensure all the error messages are in the expected language.\n\n\n",
      "cMarkNode": {}
    },
    "createdTimestamp": {
      "seconds": 1769190399,
