    "author": "model",
    "payload": {
      "type": "thinking",
      "text": "**Analyzing the Network Error**\n\nI'm currently investigating a \"TypeError: Failed to fetch\" error encountered while syncing with Supabase. I believe the user's environment might be a sandbox that restricts direct network calls, just as in the prompt's provided environment. I am now trying to confirm the assumptions.\n\n\n**Refining the Error Handling**\n\nI'm now focusing on refining the error handling in `Disciplinas.tsx`. Given the `TypeError: Failed to fetch`, I'll start by checking if it's a network error using the `isNetworkError` utility. Instead of just logging the error, I'll provide user-friendly feedback, perhaps an alert, if Supabase is unavailable. The goal is graceful fallback using `localStorage` for offline use, especially in this sandbox context. This is happening specifically within the `handleSubjectUpdate` function.\n\n\n**Implementing Local Storage Fallback**\n\nI'm now implementing the local storage fallback strategy. If Supabase is unavailable, I'll persist data locally to prevent data loss. This involves checking the Supabase connection globally in `App.tsx` and ensuring the client in `lib/supabase.ts` is initialized correctly or handles failures. I will be wrapping `supabase.from(...).update(...)` calls in `Disciplinas.tsx` with try-catch blocks that specifically check for `isNetworkError`, treating the app as in \"Offline/Local Mode\" if a network error occurs.\n\n\n**Strengthening Supabase Integration**\n\nI'm now focusing on strengthening the Supabase integration. Specifically, I will make `lib/supabase.ts` more resilient. I'll also modify `App.tsx` to handle empty or null data more gracefully when Supabase encounters an error. Looking at the user's error, it comes directly from the `handleSubjectUpdate` function in `Disciplinas.tsx`. I intend to update this to handle the error silently if it's a network issue.\n\n\n",
      "cMarkNode": {}
