      "type": "thinking",
      "text": "**Addressing the TypeScript Error**\n\nI'm currently focused on resolving the TypeScript error in `App.tsx`. The core issue appears to be a type mismatch, specifically with the props being passed down. I'm carefully reviewing the prop types and trying to ensure a clean assignment for the `{ user, users, setUsers, editais, setEditais, view }` object.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Prop Mismatch**\n\nI've homed in on the critical prop discrepancy. The error pinpoints that `AdminProps` in `Admin.tsx` doesn't define the `editais` and `setEditais` props, yet I'm passing them in `App.tsx`. After tracing, I'm examining if these props are needed in `Admin.tsx` or if the assignment in `App.tsx` requires adjustment. The intention of `view` is 'users' in this instance.\n\n\n**Clarifying Unused Props**\n\nI've confirmed my suspicion: the `Admin` component doesn't actually utilize the `editais` or `setEditais` props, even though they're being passed. The implementation exclusively focuses on user management. I'll need to figure out why they were included in the first place, or if they should be filtered out. Also, I'm checking out the gemini service file to ensure it's up to par.\n\n\n**Verifying the Gemini Service**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the `geminiService.ts` file. I've confirmed that I'm correctly employing `gemini-3-pro-preview` for complex text tasks, and using the correct data types and property access (`response.text`). I'm also ensuring the correct `responseMimeType` of `\"application/json\"` and `responseSchema` is in use. Interestingly, I've observed that the text model doesn't explicitly mandate the same API key selection dialog as the image models.\n\n\n**Refining the Prop Handling**\n\nI've determined I need to adjust the prop passing in `App.tsx`. The `editais` and `setEditais` props are superfluous in `Admin.tsx` right now, so I'll remove them from the `App.tsx` component. This resolves the TypeScript error cleanly. I also need to make sure that there are no guideline violations and perform a final check of the Gemini Service file, where I see everything correctly implemented.\n\n\n**Adjusting Unnecessary Props**\n\nI've decided to refine the `App.tsx` component further. Upon revisiting line 159, I'll remove the `editais` and `setEditais` props when rendering the `Admin` component, as they are not needed by the `Admin` component. In addition, I will double-check the gemini service file for compliance with best practices, specifically about content generation. Finally, I've confirmed that the `response.text` property acts as a getter.\n\n\n**Confirming AI Integration**\n\nI'm now verifying the integration with GenAI. The `generateContent` call uses the correct model: `\"gemini-3-pro-preview\"`. I've confirmed proper imports of `GoogleGenAI` and `Type` from `@google/genai`. Also, I'm checking that the `Ciclos` component is not rendering inside the main block in `App.tsx`.\n\n\n**Integrating the `Ciclos` Component**\n\nI've realized that the `Ciclos` component, related to the AI mentor, wasn't included in the conditional rendering logic of `App.tsx`. After reviewing the sidebar items, I found that \"Ciclos\" (Mentoria IA) isn't even in the menu, indicating a missing link. I'll need to figure out how to integrate it. The component uses `generateStudyCycle` which depends on `\"gemini-3-pro-preview\"`. I have to fix it.\n\n\n**Implementing the Component**\n\nI'm now integrating the `Ciclos` component into the application's flow. Based on the XML changes, I've cleaned up `App.tsx` and removed the superfluous props to the `Admin` component. The next step is getting the `Ciclos` component, related to the AI mentor, to render appropriately based on the user's selection, which now depends on checking for the existence of `view` 'ciclos'. I will double-check the integration with GenAI and the menu structure.\n\n\n**Incorporating Data Structures**\n\nI'm now incorporating several state variables to manage application data. I'm setting up state for `currentPage`, `subjects`, `mocks`, `cycle`, `studyLogs`, `allUsers`, and a few boolean flags like `isLoaded`, `isSidebarOpen`, `isLoggingOut`, `isOffline`, and `isDarkMode`. The plan is to create robust data management for a comprehensive user experience. Also, I'm integrating a heartbeat with Supabase to keep user presence up-to-date.\n\n\n**Managing Data Updates**\n\nI'm now implementing the user presence heartbeat with Supabase, calling `updatePresence` every two minutes. I've integrated `useEffect` with a cleanup function to clear the interval on unmount. I'm also fetching user-specific data upon component mount, using `useCallback` to avoid re-renders, and making parallel requests for subjects, mocks, study logs, and the study cycle using `Promise.all`.\n\n\n**Fetching User Data**\n\nI'm now fetching user-specific data upon component mount. I'm using `useCallback` to prevent unnecessary re-renders. This ensures efficient data retrieval and updates. Specifically, I'm fetching subjects, mocks, study logs, and the study cycle, using `Promise.all` for parallel execution. Also, I'm updating user presence and handling potential offline scenarios.\n\n\n**Implementing User Updates**\n\nI'm now integrating the data update logic within the component. When a new user logs in, I will fetch user-specific data using `fetchUserData` while also updating the user state with `setUser`. If the user has an 'administrator' role, I will fetch and update `allUsers` data. In addition, I have incorporated a logout flow to properly sign out the user, updating the UI accordingly.\n\n\n\n**Refining Sidebar Integration**\n\nI'm currently working to integrate the \"Disciplinas\" sidebar item, related to editais, into the menu structure, since I am now rendering the sidebar in a way that is sensitive to its items. I'm now making sure that the props passed from the main component are correctly assigned to the sidebar component. This involves reviewing conditional rendering logic and making sure the editais data is correctly passed and displayed. I'm also now adjusting the user role checks.\n\n\n**Adjusting Role-Based Access**\n\nNow, I'm adapting the sidebar menu structure based on user roles, since I can see the structure is tied to the `user.role` variable. I'm filtering items to show only those accessible to the current user's role, and updating the application to handle these items based on role. I'm carefully reviewing the logic to ensure that an administrator will only see the \"Membros\" and \"Matrizes\" options.\n\n\n\n**Refining Component State**\n\nI'm now integrating the `currentPage` state variable to conditionally render components in `App.tsx`. The menu structure and component display will now update dynamically based on the current page selected. I've removed the extra `editais` and `setEditais` props when calling the `Admin` component and ensured that the menu is updated to match the sidebar items. I'm focusing on user-specific component rendering and also the use of conditional rendering.\n\n\n**Verifying API Usage**\n\nI've re-examined the Gemini integration, focusing on API compliance. The code doesn't import any deprecated types and correctly uses `GoogleGenAI` and the `generateContent` method from the SDK, while using the correct `model`, `responseMimeType`, and `responseSchema`. I have also verified that the response is being handled correctly. This appears to satisfy the concerns.\n\n\n\n**Integrating the 'Ciclos' Component**\n\nI'm now integrating the `Ciclos` component, related to the AI mentor, into the navigation and conditional rendering logic. I've located the missing item in the navigation structure. I'm focusing on ensuring it renders correctly based on the user's selection and will be implemented within the main block in `App.tsx` and tied to the `view` property. I'm also ensuring the correct logic of the other menu items.\n\n\n**Removing Unnecessary Props**\n\nI've decided to refine the `App.tsx` component further, and have removed the `editais` and `setEditais` props from the `Admin` component call on line 159, as these aren't utilized within the component's internal logic. Also, I've re-examined the code, confirming that my handling of `response.text` is safe, as it correctly handles potential undefined values. The `geminiService.ts` file utilizes `gemini-3-pro-preview`.\n\n\n",
