    "payload": {
      "type": "thinking",
      "text": "**Diagnosing Recursive Loops**\n\nI'm currently focused on an \"infinite recursion detected\" error, stemming from the 'profiles' relation when attempting to save a \"Matriz.\" This looks like a Supabase/Postgres Row Level Security (RLS) issue. I believe that recursive loops are to blame. I'm investigating how the policy logic might be creating this looping behavior and preventing the save operation.\n\n\n**Unraveling Policy Dependencies**\n\nI'm now honing in on the link between the `predefined_editais` table and the `profiles` table, wondering why saving a matrix triggers a `profiles` error. It seems like the RLS policy on `predefined_editais` might be checking the user's role, leading to a subquery on `profiles`. That subquery, if it also triggers `profiles` RLS, could cause the loop. I'm focusing on the RLS policy for this.\n\n\n**Addressing API Interaction**\n\nI've shifted my focus from RLS policies to the code itself. Since I can't directly alter the database policies, I'm examining the `saveEdital` logic for potential adjustments or error handling. My analysis reveals a connection, and the \"infinite recursion\" error's reference to `profiles` strongly suggests the issue lies in the RLS policies for `predefined_editais`. I am now looking to see if there is bad usage of `profiles` in `predefined_editais`, and seeing if this is the case.\n\n\n**Isolating the Policy Loop**\n\nI've homed in on the broken RLS policy for the `profiles` table. The error message confirms an infinite recursion within that policy. It seems the issue arises when saving `predefined_editais` due to a recursive check within the policy on `profiles`. My analysis is focused on how the policy on `predefined_editais` might trigger the `profiles` RLS policy, leading to the loop. From a frontend perspective, I need to look at if we are doing unnecessary selects that trigger RLS, or ensuring that the user profile data used for role-based checks is sound.\n\n\n**Pinpointing RLS Trigger**\n\nI've discovered a possible cause! The `handleCreateUser` function directly updates the `profiles` table after user signup. This triggers the `profiles` RLS policy, especially for administrators. My focus is now on the `saveEdital` function, where a `.select().single()` call after an insert might be the culprit. I am checking if this `.select().single()` call in the `saveEdital` function triggers recursive policy evaluation.\n\n\n**Refining Query Strategy**\n\nI've zeroed in on the problematic `.select().single()` call after the insert into `predefined_editais`. My hypothesis is that if the `predefined_editais` RLS policy depends on `profiles`, and `profiles` has a recursive policy, this select causes the recursion. Removing the `.select().single()` might be a quick fix. However, I must ensure I can still return the new ID for the `edital`. I will attempt to remove the `.select().single()` and rely on a local state update, or attempt to refine the query if applicable, so I can continue to retrieve the newly created `edital`. I am also considering ways of updating the Admin.tsx file.\n\n\n**Confirming Database Policy**\n\nI'm now certain the core problem stems from a database RLS policy on the `profiles` table. The `saveEdital` operation triggers it indirectly, leading to the infinite recursion error. I have a hunch that the policy on `predefined_editais` might be the root cause, but this also means that I should check the existing policy on `profiles`. My analysis is focusing on the administrator policy, ensuring it is properly structured to avoid recursion and permit actions based on the user's role.\n\n\n**Refactoring Client-Side Logic**\n\nI'm now focusing on modifying the `Admin.tsx` file and assessing the impact on the client-side code, particularly the `saveEdital` function. I'll remove the problematic `.select().single()` call after the insert. I'll aim to create a simpler save logic and enhanced error handling. I'll add a UI hint that informs of the policy fix (which will also prevent this from occurring again). I am also checking `App.tsx` logic for similar issues in the `fetchData` logic, which could also cause this.\n\n\n**Optimizing Frontend Queries**\n\nI'm now implementing the plan to mitigate the RLS recursion error. My plan is to make the `Admin.tsx` `saveEdital` more resilient, and I'm updating it by modifying the `saveEdital` function. Specifically, I'm refactoring the `saveEdital` function to attempt the database insert, and if it fails, I'm going to perform the insert without any `.select()` call as a fallback. I'll then fetch the data to display. I'll also add enhanced error handling. I'm reviewing `types.ts` and `supabase.ts` for related improvements.\n\n\n**Analyzing User Registration**\n\nI've discovered the root cause of the recursion! The `Admin.tsx` file's `signUp` and subsequent `profiles` update is the culprit. When an admin signs up a user, the `profiles` update can trigger the recursive RLS policy. I'm focusing on making the `saveUserUpdate` more defensive. I'll add a troubleshooting hint to the UI. I will also be removing unnecessary re-renders in `Admin.tsx`.\n\n\n",
