    "author": "model",
    "payload": {
      "type": "thinking",
      "text": "**Analyzing Infinite Recursion**\n\nI'm currently focused on the root cause of infinite recursion within the RLS policies for the `profiles` table. I'm carefully analyzing the SQL code and the application context of StudyFlow, our study planning and control platform for public exams, to pinpoint the recursive loop's origin. It's a senior full stack engineer's perspective here, diving deep into the infrastructure.\n\n\n**Unraveling Recursive Access**\n\nI've zeroed in on the interplay between the `predefined_editais` table and the `profiles` table's RLS policies. The `exists` clause within \"Admins gerenciam o catálogo\" seems to be the culprit. Specifically, the problematic `profiles` table RLS queries itself to check administrator status, resulting in a recursive loop when accessed from `predefined_editais`, and causing the infinite recursion error. I'm investigating how these table policies intertwine.\n\n\n**Addressing Recursion & Structure**\n\nI'm now focusing on two key areas. First, I'm recommending switching from the problematic `exists` clause in the RLS policies to using `auth.jwt()` claims or a simpler method to check roles. Second, I am recommending improving the data structure of the `predefined_editais` table, considering a UUID or auto-incrementing integer for the primary key `id`, which may prevent potential future issues.\n\n\n**Refining Role Checks**\n\nI'm now implementing the solution. I'll provide an improved version of the SQL and the necessary app-side updates, specifically to refine `Admin.tsx` further to handle errors, and make sure the app handles profile roles correctly. My focus is on creating a \"clean\" SQL version to avoid common pitfalls, and I'll include the necessary updates to the app to handle the role checks.\n\n\n",
      "cMarkNode": {}
    },
    "createdTimestamp": {
      "seconds": 1769219467,
      "nanos": 510000000
    },
