    "id": 648,
    "author": "model",
    "payload": {
      "type": "thinking",
      "text": "**Focusing Login Security**\n\nI'm currently concentrating on enhancing the users page, specifically within the administrator mode. I've been examining ways to reinforce login security. I've got the blocked/suspended users access denial working, complete with an alert. Next, I am implementing brute-force protection, blocking users after five failed password attempts and generating an alert for blocked users.\n\n\n**Implementing Password Reset**\n\nI've added a contact link for password resets to improve accessibility. I'm now updating `Admin.tsx` and `Login.tsx` to integrate the brute-force protection, incorporating logic to manage failed login attempts and user status updates (blocked/active). The database is being updated to include a `failed_attempts` column in the `profiles` table. The administrator will be able to see the status, manually block users, and optionally view the failed attempts counter. The login screen will block login attempts for blocked users, and display helpful specific error messages to guide the user.\n\n\n**Refining UI and Functionality**\n\nI'm focusing on UI improvements and ensuring the login process correctly handles blocked users. If a user is blocked, they will be signed out immediately, and \"Conta Suspensa\" will be displayed. I'm updating `Admin.tsx` to include clearer status indicators and a more robust delete button. I'm also ensuring that resetting user attempts occurs when an admin edits or unblocks a user. The database fix is being updated to reflect `failed_attempts` column changes. I need to make sure the state for failed attempts UI is added to `Login.tsx`.\n\n\n**Analyzing Login Flow**\n\nI'm now carefully analyzing the login flow to integrate status checks before the `signInWithPassword` call. I realized the importance of checking user status (blocked or not) before attempting authentication. My logic will ensure this is handled, and I'll increment failed login counters. I must also consider potential database query failures with RLS and adjust accordingly. I'm keeping aesthetics in mind, aiming for a \"Master UI\" / Cyberpunk / High Contrast design.\n\n\n",
      "cMarkNode": {}
    },
    "createdTimestamp": {
