      "type": "thinking",
      "text": "**Investigating Missing Sidebar**\n\nI'm now focusing on the 'deconfigured' state the admin user is encountering after login, specifically the missing left sidebar. It seems the issue stems from recent changes made to `Admin.tsx` and `Login.tsx` concerning security enhancements, the admin guard in particular. I'm investigating how these modifications might be inadvertently impacting the rendering of the sidebar.\n\n\n**Analyzing App Role Handling**\n\nI'm now diving into `App.tsx` and how it handles the admin role and page states, particularly after reviewing `Admin.tsx`. The sidebar logic is now under scrutiny, focusing on role filtering and how `currentPage` impacts rendering. I'm noting the initial value of `currentPage` and how the filtering by role is implemented within the sidebar. This may provide critical insight.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Role-Based Rendering**\n\nI'm now focusing on `App.tsx` and the user role logic. The sidebar should render if a `user` exists, but the issue is the sidebar is missing after login. I'm scrutinizing conditional rendering based on `currentPage`. Even though the user is logging in, either the CSS, state variables or conditional rendering in `App.tsx` may be the issue, or `Login.tsx` doesn't seem to make the login screen go away. I'm verifying the `ralysonriccelli@gmail.com` logic in `Login.tsx` to confirm the user is assigned the 'administrator' role.\n\n\n**Examining Sidebar Visibility**\n\nI'm now focusing on `App.tsx` and the sidebar's conditional rendering. The sidebar's visibility hinges on `isSidebarOpen` and `lg:` classes. It seems the issue is more likely in the `App.tsx` layout rather than the roles, especially because the user mentions the *sidebar* is missing. The previous code contained a hardcoded return, but this appears to be the problem in the `App.tsx` layout. I'm prioritizing `isSidebarOpen` and the responsiveness classes.\n\n\n**Evaluating Page Redirection**\n\nI'm now focusing on the login redirection process and how the `currentPage` state is managed in `App.tsx`, and how that interacts with the user. It seems the \"deconfigured\" state might occur after login, leading to an incorrect page render. I'm scrutinizing the `useEffect` within `App.tsx`, especially how user roles are handled and the initial page loaded.\n\n\n**Refining Component Rendering**\n\nI'm now shifting gears to `Admin.tsx`. I believe the \"deconfigured\" state likely involves the Admin views. I plan to address the placeholder in the `Admin.tsx` code for Editais, ensuring the same UI for both users and editais, while checking that `App.tsx` layout is not the problem. Then, I'm focusing on the sidebar again, since the prompt says \"o menu lateral esquerdo não aparece\". I am now checking to see if `isSidebarOpen` is the problem in `App.tsx`, or a CSS conflict; I'm considering responsive classes, which depend on screen width. Finally, I'm returning to `Admin.tsx`.\n\n\n**Diagnosing Rendering Issues**\n\nI'm now focusing on the interaction of `Admin.tsx` and `App.tsx`. Specifically, I'm concerned that an error in `Admin.tsx` can cause the entire application to unmount. The 'deconfigured' look makes me think that something is wrong. I'm focusing on the `isLoaded` state in `App.tsx`, which seems to render a loading screen. I now remember that in `Admin.tsx`, when `view` is 'editais', almost nothing is rendered.\n\n\n**Restoring Core Functionality**\n\nI'm now implementing the plan. First, I'm restoring the \"Editais\" view with full functionality in `Admin.tsx`, ensuring UI consistency with the \"Users\" view. Then, I'll update `App.tsx` to separate sidebar and layout responsively, with an admin role check. I'm also addressing the \"Access Denied\" return in `Admin.tsx` to prevent any layout shifts. I am now prioritizing the changes to `App.tsx`. I believe `Admin.tsx` isn't the problem, and that `App.tsx` has logic that relies on `ralysonriccelli@gmail.com` to pass a check.\n\n\n**Confirming Role Assignment**\n\nI've hit a breakthrough! I realized a crucial mismatch. The `Login.tsx` file checks for the admin email hardcoded, but `App.tsx` did not have that logic. When the user logs in, `App.tsx` may initially assign the 'student' role. I'll rectify this in `App.tsx` to include the same fallback admin check, ensuring the admin role persists after login. I'm confident this solves the apparent menu disappearance and will address the \"deconfigured\" look, since the user is in the wrong role. I'm now implementing the fix!\n\n\n",
