  {
    "id": 668,
    "author": "model",
    "payload": {
      "type": "thinking",
      "text": "**Refining Deletion Logic**\n\nOkay, I've got the deletion process working in the UI; deleted users vanish from the React state, which is a start. My next challenge is ensuring that the data in Supabase accurately reflects the UI changes. Currently, deleted users persist in the database. I'm focusing on modifying the `Admin.tsx` and related components to include a function to physically delete the user rows from Supabase, so the data is consistent.\n\n\n**Addressing Database Cleanup**\n\nOkay, I'm now honing in on the database cleanup. It seems the `profiles` table deletions don't cascade to `auth.users`, which makes sense. I need to handle this manually. I'm focusing on strengthening the delete calls within `Admin.tsx` with error handling and awaiting the Supabase call. I will then ensure the frontend user states are aligned with the backend in `App.tsx`. I'm starting to think through the best approach to ensure only active or blocked users persist.\n\n\n**Implementing Full Deletion**\n\nI'm now implementing the full deletion process. I'm focusing on providing methods to completely remove a user from the system. First, I'll modify `Admin.tsx` to properly delete from the `profiles` table. Next, I'll create SQL to synchronize `auth.users` and `profiles`. Finally, in `App.tsx`, I'll add a check to boot \"ghost\" users. The goal is to ensure the database accurately reflects active and blocked users only, as desired.\n\n\n",
      "cMarkNode": {}
    },
    "createdTimestamp": {
      "seconds": 1769268856,
      "nanos": 706000000
    },
    "doneTimestamp": {
