    "id": 990,
    "author": "model",
    "payload": {
      "type": "thinking",
      "text": "**Refining Study Cycles**\n\nI'm now focusing on how to manipulate study cycles for maximum efficiency. I'm exploring the option of swapping subjects between cycles, for example, shifting \"Admin Pública\" from Cycle 1 to \"Licitações\" in Cycle 2. Furthermore, I'm considering the manual removal of a subject from a particular cycle.\n\n\n**Implementing Manual Additions**\n\nI'm now adding functionality to manually include subjects within specific study cycles. The primary focus is on how users can manipulate the study plan directly. Thinking about how the system should handle this, I need to integrate user actions with the underlying data structure efficiently. Current approaches include a \"Transfer\" mode for subject manipulation, or an \"Edit Mode\" which simplifies the addition and removal of subjects, all while linking back to data persistence.\n\n\n**Designing UI Interactions**\n\nI'm now deep in designing the user interface interactions for subject management. I've defined \"Trash\" and \"Swap\" icons for direct control. The swapping logic will involve a temporary \"sourceSubject\" state. \"Edit\" buttons will appear on cycle card headers, along with specific functions such as a \"remove\", \"move/swap\" and an \"add\" button. For manual additions, a dropdown menu is planned. The UI component resides in `components/Ciclos.tsx`, and the `activeCycles` type needs an update. I'm focusing on updating the application state and data persistence through `activeCycles` and `persistChanges` respectively.\n\n\n",
      "cMarkNode": {}
    },
    "createdTimestamp": {
      "seconds": 1769417110,
      "nanos": 907000000
    },
    "doneTimestamp": {
